.TH "WEBVTT" "1" "Oct 18, 2026" "0.5.1" "webvtt-py"
.SH NAME
webvtt \- segment and shift WebVTT captions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
webvtt segment PATH \-o OUTPUT [\-d NUMBER] [\-m NUMBER]
webvtt shift PATH OFFSET [\-o OUTPUT] [\-s TIMESTAMP]
webvtt \-h
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
\fBwebvtt\fP segments a WebVTT caption file for \X'tty: link https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-pantos-hls-rfc8216bis'\fI\%HTTP Live Streaming\fP\X'tty: link' (HLS)
and shifts the timestamps of its captions.
.sp
\fBsegment\fP splits the captions in \fBPATH\fP into segments of a target duration. Each
segment is written to \fBOUTPUT\fP as \fBfileSequenceN.webvtt\fP, where \fBN\fP is
the segment index starting at 0, along with a playlist named
\fBprog_index.m3u8\fP that lists all the segments.
.sp
A caption whose time span crosses a segment boundary is included in every
segment it overlaps, so players display it regardless of which segment they
load.
.sp
Every segment starts with an \fBX\-TIMESTAMP\-MAP\fP header that maps the local
caption time \fB00:00:00.000\fP to an MPEG\-TS presentation timestamp, so the
captions are kept in sync with the video stream.
.sp
\fBshift\fP moves the captions in \fBPATH\fP earlier or later by \fBOFFSET\fP
seconds. The offset is rounded to the nearest millisecond and a timestamp
that would fall before zero is set to \fB00:00:00.000\fP, so a caption moved
entirely before the start is kept with \fB00:00:00.000 \-\-> 00:00:00.000\fP
timings. The shifted captions are written to standard output in UTF\-8, or to
\fBOUTPUT\fP when given.
.SH COMMANDS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBsegment\fP
Segment the WebVTT file at \fBPATH\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fBshift\fP
Shift the timestamps of the WebVTT file at \fBPATH\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SH OPTIONS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Show the help message and exit. Also available for each command, as in
\fBwebvtt shift \-h\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS Segment options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\-o OUTPUT\fP, \fB\-\-output OUTPUT\fP
Directory where the segments and playlist are written. It is created if
it does not exist. Existing files with the same names are overwritten.
Required.
.TP
.B \fB\-d NUMBER\fP, \fB\-\-target\-duration NUMBER\fP
Target duration of each segment in seconds. Also used for the
\fB#EXT\-X\-TARGETDURATION\fP and \fB#EXTINF\fP values of the playlist.
Default: \fB10\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB\-m NUMBER\fP, \fB\-\-mpegts NUMBER\fP
MPEG\-TS value written to the \fBX\-TIMESTAMP\-MAP\fP header of each segment.
Default: \fB900000\fP (10 seconds at the 90 kHz MPEG\-TS clock).
.UNINDENT
.SS Shift options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBOFFSET\fP
Number of seconds to shift the captions by, such as \fB2\fP or \fB0.25\fP\&.
Negative values move the captions earlier, as in \fB\-1.5\fP\&. Required.
.TP
.B \fB\-o OUTPUT\fP, \fB\-\-output OUTPUT\fP
File where the shifted captions are written, keeping the encoding and
Byte Order Mark of the input file. It can be the input file itself to
shift it in place. A \fB\&.vtt\fP extension is added if missing, and if it
is a directory the file is written there with the input file name.
Default: standard output.
.TP
.B \fB\-s TIMESTAMP\fP, \fB\-\-start TIMESTAMP\fP
Only shift the captions starting at or after \fBTIMESTAMP\fP, in
\fB[hh:]mm:ss.mmm\fP format. Default: shift all the captions.
.UNINDENT
.SH EXIT STATUS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB0\fP
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B \fB1\fP
The input file could not be read or is not a valid WebVTT file, or the
output could not be written. An error message is printed to
standard error, for example:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
webvtt: error: captions.vtt: not a valid WebVTT file
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fB2\fP
Invalid command line arguments, such as an offset that is not a number
or an invalid \fB\-\-start\fP timestamp.
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
Given \fBcaptions.vtt\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 \-\-> 00:00:04.000
Hello and welcome.

00:00:06.000 \-\-> 00:00:12.000
This caption spans two segments.

00:00:14.500 \-\-> 00:00:18.000
Thanks for watching!
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Segment it into 10 second segments:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ webvtt segment captions.vtt \-\-output out
$ ls out
fileSequence0.webvtt  fileSequence1.webvtt  prog_index.m3u8
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBout/prog_index.m3u8\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
#EXTM3U
#EXT\-X\-TARGETDURATION:10
#EXT\-X\-VERSION:3
#EXT\-X\-PLAYLIST\-TYPE:VOD
#EXTINF:10.00000,
fileSequence0.webvtt
#EXTINF:10.00000,
fileSequence1.webvtt
#EXT\-X\-ENDLIST
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBout/fileSequence0.webvtt\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
WEBVTT
X\-TIMESTAMP\-MAP=MPEGTS:900000,LOCAL:00:00:00.000

00:00:01.000 \-\-> 00:00:04.000
Hello and welcome.

00:00:06.000 \-\-> 00:00:12.000
This caption spans two segments.
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBout/fileSequence1.webvtt\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
WEBVTT
X\-TIMESTAMP\-MAP=MPEGTS:900000,LOCAL:00:00:00.000

00:00:06.000 \-\-> 00:00:12.000
This caption spans two segments.

00:00:14.500 \-\-> 00:00:18.000
Thanks for watching!
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Use 6 second segments and a different MPEG\-TS value:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ webvtt segment captions.vtt \-o out \-d 6 \-m 0
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Show the captions 1.5 seconds earlier:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ webvtt shift captions.vtt \-1.5
WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 \-\-> 00:00:02.500
Hello and welcome.

00:00:04.500 \-\-> 00:00:10.500
This caption spans two segments.

00:00:13.000 \-\-> 00:00:16.500
Thanks for watching!
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Show the last caption 2 seconds later, updating the file in place:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ webvtt shift captions.vtt 2 \-\-start 00:14.500 \-o captions.vtt
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH SEE ALSO
.sp
The same segmentation and shifting are available from Python with
\fBwebvtt.segment()\fP and \fBWebVTT.shift()\fP\&.
